93 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 77 0 R
/E (Stimulus: Muscle gain begins with a stimulus, typically in the form of resistance training or strength training exercises. When you lift weights or perform resistance exercises, you create small tears in your muscle fibers. This stimulus signals your body to repair and strengthen the damaged muscle tissue.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
94 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 77 0 R
/E (Repair and Regeneration: Following the workout, the body initiates the process of muscle repair and regeneration. Satellite cells, located around muscle fibers, become activated and multiply in response to the damage. These satellite cells then fuse with existing muscle fibers or form new muscle fibers, leading to muscle growth and repair.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
95 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 77 0 R
/E (Protein Synthesis: Protein synthesis is a crucial component of muscle gain. After exercise, the body increases the rate of protein synthesis, which is the process by which amino acids are assembled into new muscle proteins. This elevated protein synthesis helps repair damaged muscle fibers and promotes muscle growth.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
96 0 obj
<<
