101 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 79 0 R
/E (Progressive overload and hypertrophy are fundamental concepts in strength training. Progressive overload means gradually increasing the demands placed on your muscles over time. This can be achieved by lifting heavier weights, doing more repetitions, or increasing the number of sets during workouts. Essentially, you're continually challenging your muscles to adapt to new levels of stress. Hypertrophy, on the other hand, refers to the increase in muscle size that occurs as a result of progressive overload. When you consistently challenge your muscles with progressively heavier weights or increased intensity, they respond by becoming stronger and larger. In simpler terms, progressive overload is like giving your muscles a harder puzzle to solve each time you work out, and hypertrophy is the muscle's way of growing bigger and stronger to meet that challenge.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
102 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 79 0 R
/T (Training For Gaining Muscle)
/E (Training For Gaining Muscle)
